Royal Banks of Scotland Are Disapprecting From Their Clients Out of Scotland
According to RBS, the bank's operations outside Scotland will be curtailed in such a way that in the future it will only provide business services, i.e. back-office services.
The Royal Bank of Scotland disappears from its customers outside of Scotland.
Ross McEwan told public broadcaster BBC Scotland that the RBS brand was originally linked to the bank's global goals.
The bank was forced to back down from its goals when it almost plummeted eight years ago and had to be saved.
At that time, brand strategists have used RBS to protect other consumer financing brands.
It sponsors millions of pounds of international sport such as Six Nations rugby and Wimbledon champion Andy Murray.
But it has now been considered right to put more national brands at the forefront.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will remain available to Scottish customers, but there won't be an abbreviation RBS.
In England and Wales, all RBS names will be transformed into NatWeste, with the exception of head office and a stock exchange listing.
The Ulster Bank brand is already in use in the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland.
In addition, there will be other smaller private banking brands that will gain greater stipulation, such as Coutts, Adam & Co, Drummond and Holt's Military Bank.
McEwan was interviewed during a presentation for customers and staff in Inverness-shire.
He told BBC Scotland: "RBS is transformed into an investment brand and it's because we're now transforming into a bank of a wide variety of brands.
Because the bank itself became a global brand, RBS also became a global brand.
Now our acquests are no longer global, they are local.
Each of these brands represents something very different in its own community, and our employees work with customers under these brands.”
RBS has already announced it will stop sponsoring the Six Nations rugby, elevating the profile of various brands in sports sponsorship.
"It's the right time to move into a bank with different brands, because basically it's a matter of how we're going to be able to make a bank better for customers," says the bank's president.
" Three years ago it would have been very squeaking if we'd say we're going to be an excellent bank for our customers and introduced brands.
But the work that we're doing and focusing on customer needs instead of our own needs have changed a lot.
We can bring those brands back again, so I think it's the right time.'
