Pok-mon Go yacht Brings Health and Happiness
It's fascinating to see how the world is changing.
Over the past decade, many have complained about why teenagers and children don't go out to play anymore, prefer to stay inside to stare at their screens.
Pokemon Go makes people get off the couch and get moving.
Originally, screen time was taken only by watching the television, then video games and, most recently, smartphones.
So it's great to see that technology and outdoor sports aren't mutually exclusive.
Pok-mon Go is a game application whose characters come from a video game created in the 1990s (of which was also made into a series of drawings, playing cards and other ancillary products).
Similar to a lot of popular cultural phenomena, this stopped shortly before a new game was made available in the United States, Australia and New Zealand at the beginning of July.
Lanting in other countries was delayed due to huge interest that overburdened servers.
In just a matter of a matter of days, the number of users in Pok-mon Go overtosked Tinder and was almost as big as on Twitter.
So, how does the game work?
Brief-indicated, players go out looking for Pok-mon on their smartphones, which are tracked using GPS. When found in the pok-mon, it can be "photographed" (using the phone's display image).
The craze has snatched a generation who played a video game when he's younger and lifted his buttocks off the couch.
In the U.S., the game has significantly increased the number of steps taken by "medium person" in a day.
The Washington Post recently reported on the Cardiogram app of the Apple Watch smartwatch, which detected within two days of the game's launch that the number of 30-minute-to-day trajectories rose abruptly from 45% to 53%. Cardiogram follows 35,000 users.
The company doesn't know which of the users is playing Pok-mon Go, it sees only the raw data of exercise volumes.
A number of public health promotions encouraging people to get off the couch and tweaking for at least 30 minutes a day have relegated to debaptical because campaigns have been dulled and their advertising characters have looked unhappy.
But the importance of physical activity shouldn't be underestimated.
Regular people are less probabilistic to be diagnosed with cancer or depression.
Exerciliation helps with weight control, reduces blood pressure, prevents diabetes and brings a large amount of other benefits.
The fancy of Pokemon Go is to get people out to do something they're enjoying.
In this way, it achieves three basic health-enhancing basic things – exercise, fresh air and sunlight and joy.
It's even possible to make new friends during yacht.
As usual, those in this phenomenon resentful joy-kickers.
And yes, when they play, someone can walk to the door.
How about it?
A break-through is done.
However, while smartphone apps like Fitbit could monitor your activity earlier, they didn't give you a reason to get going, as Pok-mon Go does.
The novelty is disappearing, and usage will decrease in due course.
Thanks to the success of Pokemon, however, there will be more games combining applications and virtual realities with activity in the real world, which will increase the physical activity of people going to the yacht and improve their fitness and health.
