"Revisive results approved by Centcom's senior executives generally gave a more positive picture of US counter-terror irrigations than the facts on the ground showed, and were invariably more positive than in the analyses of other units in the reconnaissance community," according to a press release on the report.
“Centcom events cannot be accepted – our soldiers are affected when false analyses are presented to high-level policy makers,” California Republican Rep. Ken Calvert said.
"Centcom's management errors extend to an organization's top level."
A 10-page report detailing the continuing problems in 2014-2015 that Central Command's depictions and analyses of American efforts to educating Iraqi troops.
Although the report contains no consequential evidence of falsified reports commissioned by Obama's administrative authorities, it gives an image of analysts to the notion that Centcom's leaders have pressured them to present a more optimistic view of the threat posed by ISIS posed.
"In the first half of 2015, many Central Command's press releases, statements and witness statements given to Congress were significantly more positive than what actually happened," the report said.
"For example, a Centcom official issued a public statement that a major military assault to reclaim Mosul could take Mosul as early as early as April or May 2015.
Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, remains under ISIS control.
