" Between 2014 and 2015, Centcom created an overcontinuous process of assessing intelligence on ISIS and Iraqi security forces," said Adam B. Schiff, a high-premising Democrat representative at the committee.
Schiff said this process "obtrusive releasing reviving reconnaissance results, "abstant the morality of analysts" and "taking insufficient divergent views."
Schiff and Democrats said there was no evidence that the White House was trying to pressure Centcom's analysts to adapt their conclusions to a "primary-deprecated or official policy."
Daily Beast, a news website on Tuesday, reported on the preliminary results of an investigation conducted by Congress.
Last August, the New York Times reported an inquest by the Pentagon superintendent, which was launched as a result of lawsuits filed by Centcom analysts.
Pentagon Spokesman, Lieutenant-Castor Patrick Evans, says in a statement that the Defense Department would not comment on a congressional report as long as an investigation by a superintendent is under way.
However, he says: "Experts sometimes disagree on the interpretation of ambiguities of complex information, and the intelligence community and the Ministry of Defence welcome a healthy dialogue on these key issues of national security."
There are other repercussions besides trying to find out whether U.S.-led bomb bombardments in Iraq and Syria have been successful (increasing claim made by multiple defence officials).
Last year's claims, however, summed up how much President Obama can rely on the Central Command to give honest assessments of a military operation in Iraq, Afghanistan, Libya and other crisis areas.
Critish criticism of ISIS responsive disagreements created more than a decade ago, when Centcom's intelligence analyst Gregory Hooker published an investigation according to President George W. Bush's government, despite protests from many analysts, supported only small troops into Iraq and spent little time devoting to deflection that would result from a 2003 invasion.
Hooker also played a key role in rebellion against ISIS intelligence.
According to officials last year the Hooker taskforce drew a conclusion that despite opposing public statements, airstrikes on ISIS-controlled refineries had not significantly weakened the finances of the terrorist group because it had built temporary-fashioned refineries, thus being able to sell oil on a black market.
However, the outcome of the investigation was ignored outside the Central Command, The Times reported in September.
Centcom's then commander, General Lloyd J. Austin III, was criticized last year after many legislators judged testimony to the Senate as being too positive about the war.
