Trampoline brings zen in the middle of an Olympic hulin
Irrespective of location and time, you will inevitably come across the Olympics.
The events, competitions and struggles in Rio de Janeiro seem to be followed by frantic remedication constantly and everywhere.
It's as if an Olympic fire itself were a flaming baton of truding to a swimmer to a swimmer, a swimmer to a gymnast and a gymnast to a triathletes.
You won't be able to open a TV, read a newspaper or listen to your neighbor's conversation in a cafe queue unawaited about Simone Biles, Katie Ledecky or a quarrel between Michael Phelps and Chad le Clos.
And then there's a guy named Logan Dooley.
Dooley, an Olympic first-time Olympian, charms with a smile and might well be a name on everyone's lips that could be seen and included in the best time of viewing and listening, would be preferred by sponsors and would appear in feel-yellow TV ads if he were just a swimmer, gymnast or even a swordsman.
But Dooley, 28, from Lake Forest in California, is voyage competing for Olympic gold on a trampoline.
The Olympic fire does not illuminatirate all competitions equally.
Farther from the center, in partial shade there are less well-known sports such as air pistol shooting, archery, badminton and taekwondo.
We're always going to have a breaststroke, and we'll stick to our championship for a long time.
But if I get permission to write a modest story about my own special favourite, I want to draw attention to the understatised attraction of trampolines.
Although trampoline has been an Olympic sport since the 2000 Sydney Games, its visibility over the past 16 years has been so limited that many of the people I interviewed did not even know it was an Olympic sport.
Ignorance can be region-specific.
From the United States yes, athletes have taken part in an Olympic thrall, but the country has never reached the medals and did not advance to the finals until 2012.
In the men's trampoline, medals seem to go to the Chinese and the Russians, and Canadians have scooped a medal haul on the women's side.
Dong Dong of China and Rosie MacLennan of Canada are defending the gold medals this year they defeated in London.
Trampolin, however, has underdog charm. Partially because for an unciviated ear it sounds more suitable for circus school than the Olympics.
(I recognize that I'm not an expert, just an ordinary fan.)
The sport has its comical potential used in cartoon films for endless controversy sessions of characters. But it also has the same dignity as gymnastics and swimming jumping, i.e. sports that combine in trampoline essentially.
The word trampoline comes from the Spanish word el trampol, which translates to "publind board".
Ambitious masters scurriminate with a huge trampoline stabbing them to an altitude of nearly 10 meters. Before descending on their feet, jumpers must make a series of acrobatic movements such as spirals, quests and flips.
According to the official Olympic program, the jumps will be judged based on performance, degree of difficulty and "flight time."
Thirty-two athletes from 17 different countries will compete for trampoline planning championships in Rio. Of them there will be 16 men and 16 women.
The most common direction for Olympic sports is straight ahead.
Meditary bracelets in pursuit of gold need to get further, quicker, up and over, sometimes across the finish line, at other times back and forth around the field.
In addition to the finish line or the output wall, they also have another goal that they acquainted in televised report weapons, namely a world or Olympic record.
This determined quest is lofty, admirable, even fanatic – and it's even enough to get a spectator in tears.
But I also find this a bit exhausting just when it comes to watching the Games.
A trampoline provides respirators for this.
Trampoline direction is upwards.
The goal is not a move forward.
The gymnast bounces up weightless like an astronaut, drops down and clutches back up effortlessly.
Of course, the sport also includes a competitive aspect, nor is it easy, but there's nothing to do with a frantic scramble to the finish line or jumping far, only a forgetful and smooth combination of time and gravitational.
It's as close to zen as an Olympic race can make it.
Women's qualifiers and finals will take place on Friday afternoon and start at 1pm local time and the men's respective races at the same time on Saturday.
NBC will broadcast the finals as part of an afternoon broadcast starting at 2 p.m. local time on both days.
In the midst of Olympic mania brings a peaceful and wonderful moment that, in the midst of a common buoyancy might go unnoticed.
“After an increase in awareness can only be raised by offering attractive male athletes and objectifying them – as US male gymnastenders, according to the Wall Street Journal, recently suggested, Logan Dooley is just waiting to shovel his new crowd of fans.
A whole-general protest in Ethiopia, a long-serving U.S. all-time all-around
Violent Protest in Ethiopia
Government forces forcibly dismantled protests calling for political change across Ethiopia.
Witnesses say a number of demonstrators have been fired to death in clashes with police.
Is Ethiopia disintegrating?
For the past decade it has been one of Africa's most stable countries and a trustworthy Western ally whose economy has grown rapidly.
Over the past few months, however, anti-government rallies have rattled the country that are spreading to ever wider areas.
Last week alone, thousands of people scrambled into the streets calling for a fundamental political change.
Human rights groups said the government's reaction was relentless.
Witnesses said officers fired and killed a number of unarmed demonstrators.
Videos of demonstrations presumably filmed late last year or at the beginning of early this year show security forces beating young people who have been forcened to stand with their hands against a wall.
U.N. chief human rights official is now calling for in-depth research.
"It has always been difficult to keep the country unified, and it's even harder to move forward," says Rashid Abdi, Research Director of the International Crisis Group of the African Sarvi project.
After Nigeria, Ethiopia is Africa's second-largest country with stability promoted by Western countries.
The U.S. military and intelligence are closely backing Etigians in the fight against terrorist threats across the region, especially in Somalia. Fewer African countries, if anything, receive so much help from the West.
Ethiopia's economy has grown at a rapid pace.
It's a drastically improvement in its infrastructure, in Addis Ababa, the capital, there's even a new conurbation train.
Urban revities are usually peaceful, safe and clean.
Ethiopia, however, is still a long way from a model country of democracy. Human rights groups have been constantly reporting government repressive policies, the opposition in the country is very limited and dissenting dissensitizers will be suppressed quickly.
A lot of people have been expelled, imprisoned, chased or chased far into the wilderness.
But the situation may be changing.
"If the people are suffocated and have no choice but to demonstrate, it erupts out," says Seyoum Teshome, who works as a lecturer at the University of Central Espoo.
“The whole youth is demonstrating.
A whole generation is protesting.”
There are many topics of dissatisfaction, ranging from land use to a government coalition strrang for power.
After last year's widely criticised elections, the ruling party and its allies were given the last place previously reciprocated to the Opposition, as a result of which now dominate the entire parliament.
At the same time, tensions are rising at the Eritrean border, where an exacerbated situation claimed hundreds of casualties at a fight in June.
Analysts fear that more or less defeated separatist groups in recent years, such as Oromo's liberation front or Ogaden People's Liberation Front, may seek to take advantage of turmoil and re-emerge.
There are many reasons for the bitter mood that smotched under the surface for years to erupt right now.
The first is an apparently innocent: smartphones.
It wasn't until the past couple of years that Ethiopians have been able to communicate on social media as cheaper smartphones became common and internet availability improved.
However, even when the government repeatedly blocks access to Facebook and Twitter, especially during demonstrations, many will have access to the internet through proxy servers that conceal their whereabouts.
A lot of Ethiopian youths told me this is how they summoned people for protests.
The second reason is an increase in solidarity between Ethiopia's two largest population groups of Oromas and ambers.
Oromos and Amhara are not natural allies.
Amass from Ethiopia's largely Christian highlands have long dominated the country's politics and business, deprivation of oromists, a large number of which are Muslims and live in low-rise areas.
But even this is changing.
"Collaboration is deepening and we're getting under the same umbrella," says Mulatu Gemechu, Director of Oromo.
The biggest demonstrations have been in oromo and amhara areas.
A number of Amhara and Oromos feel that Ethiopia is unjustifiably dominated by Tigre, representing only about six per cent of the population and nevertheless dominates the army, intelligence services, trade and politics.
The third cause behind the unrest is Meles Zenawi's death.
Meles, the ex-religious leader, was Ethiopia's prime minister for 17 years until he died of an unmeblished illness by name in 2012.
He was considered a tactical genius who was able to see around the corner.
Analysts say he was particularly proficient in detecting displeasance the first signs of displeasing ambassadors who reconciled relations between counterparties.
"The current government is lacking this essential instinct," says Abdi, Conflict Investigator Abdi.
Ethiopia's new Prime Minister Hailemariam Desalegn stepped into Meles' boots from almost unfamilient.
Contrary to Meles, which originated in Tigre region of northern Ethiopia, Hailemariam comes from the south.
Analysts say he does not have the confidence of security forces managed by the Tigre tribe.
A lot of people think this will mean more bloodshed.
The last time Ethiopia experienced similar riots was in 2005, with thousands protesting against the election that the government, according to analysts, first screwed and then stobbed.
In post-election unsettles, many protesters were smipped, although there were fewer deaths than in the preceding months, and unrest ceased relatively quickly.
Development experts have praised Ethiopian leaders for far-spissed infrastructure planning, such as a new built-away train, and significant advances in the fight against poverty.
But an inner resentment toward the Ethiopian government, it has clearly not prevented an intensification.
And resentment is about to take a dangerous ethnic form.
Last month, protesters in the Amhara town of Gondar attacked companies owned by tigers, and hate speech against tigres is becoming more common on social media.
Analysts say the protests put the United States and other Western allies in a fraught situation.
The U.S. U.S. government has used Ethiopia as a base for unoccupied, neighboring Somalia-observed arranging arranging airships, according to U.S.
At the same time, the West clearly wants to support democracy, it also does not want an ally in an already volatile region to collaps.
"This requires a firm balancing act," says Abdi.
Donald Trump says he was sarcastic in claiming Obama as ISIS co-founder
Donald J.T. Trump on Friday tried to pull his words back into a dispute over his latest campaign by arguing that he wasn't serious about claiming President Obama and Hillary Clinton several times this week as "founders" of terrorist group ISIS.
Clarification sparked new questions about a Republican-president's ability of a presidential nominee to communicate clearly to their nation's citizens.
An awkward Situations will also have his supporters, who have to try to defend their candidates during these turbulent moments and to explain the comments he may not have meant.
“Trump made the allegation at an election rally on Wednesday night and reiterated in various interviews on Thursday that he really meant what he said in claiming the president and Hillary Clinton created ISIS.
But early Friday morning, Trump tweeted and said his claim was sarcasming.
In a tweet, Trump completely revelled his speech from the previous day, when he said in a number of interviews that he was quite serious in his accusations.
When the radio presenter and Conservative Hugh Hewitt tried to help Trump soften his claim by stating that Obama is trying to extremize I ISIS.
The most recent whistling sprung when Trump tried to focus his campaign back on the economy as approval ratings dropped.
Trump and Clinton delivered their economic policy policy speeches in Michigan this week.
On Friday, Trump tried to revisit the topic.
On Twitter, he cautioned that Clinton's proposals would raise taxes, and found Clinton's speech bored.
Trump has defended himself with sarcasms before.
At the end of July, he received widespread criticism when he seemed to urging Russians to hack Hillary Clinton's e-mail. Later he explained his comments by saying: "Obviously I was sarcastic."
In this way, Trump is pushing the bounds of political speech and withdrawing his words if necessary.
"I think it's his habit of testing certain messages emillating an echelon from his supporters. He's looking at how supporters will respond to messages, and then "calculate" in one way or another depending on the reactions," says Ruth Sherman, a communications expert who has been observing the language used by both presidential nominees.
"But I also believe that he understands his assertions and knows they are sufficiently circumstantial to penetrate different sieves."
Instead of softening his statements to an election audience, in recent days he's changed his style even more pungent as his endorsements have continued to decline.
A new poll conducted by NBC, the Wall Street Journal and Marist on Friday reported that Trump's support is significantly lower than Clinton in North Carolina, Virginia and Colorado, while in Florida it's a tight-ffighting situation.
At the same time as Trump usually tries to avoid apologizing for provocative remarks, he often accuses his detradicators of misinterpreting his words or failing to misunderstand his sense of humour.
But having come under increasing scrutiny in the last few months of his presidential campaign, Trump's jokes have put his loyal backers in an awkward position in which they will have to publicly defend Trump's comments however provocative.
