Adblock Plus and Facebook Advertising In Cat-Mous Mouse Play
Efforts to misleasure anti-advertisement software have turned into cat-mouse games, according to a company that produces anti-retrophies.
On Thursday, just two days after Facebook announced on its own page to optimizing ad-prohibition programs, Adblock Plus posted a blog post, telling me that users can update the filters of its blocking software, thus re-obstructing social network ads.
On Tuesday, Facebook announced announculation entry codes for its desktop sites to make it harder for ad-blocking programs to identify which parts of the page are ads.
But now Adblock says he'll figure out the new codes.
Adblock spokeswoman Ben Williams wrote in a blog post that "such ballooning between an open-source ad-prohibition community and ditagonists has been running ever since ad-prohibition was invented" and that it's possible for Facebook to make its own repair shop soon again.
The fact that the world's largest social media company has taken part in the fight is a strong indication of how big a concern advertisers have developed into the industry.
An estimated 200 million people worldwide use anti-advertising programs, according to a study conducted by Pagefair and Adobe last year. The figure has nearly quadrupled since 2013.
Facebook uses 1.03 million active users on mobile devices every day, and mobile advertising already accounts for 84% of Facebook's advertising revenue.
However, the company plans to invest in diverting ad-prohibition programs in its desktop app, as a spokesman says mobile ad blocking programs don't work on Facebook's mobile apps and on Instagram.
Revenominations from desktop ads are still high enough, which is why Facebook found the focus to be profitable.
On Tuesday, Facebook also said it would update an advertising tool for user settings to allow users to better control how ads are targeted at them.
On Thursday, Facebook criticized Adblock's new filters, saying they will also prevent some ordinary Facebook postings from appearing.
"This isn't a good experience for users, and we're going to solve the problem," a Facebook spokesman said in an email announcement.
"Advantage Anti-Control programs are clunky and ineffective, and that's why we've instead invested in developing user settings such as advertising tools to help people control advertising targeting themselves."
A woman commits suicide by being hit by cars on a Swiss motorway
The victim was lying on a freeway in the middle of the night.
Police are appealing for the help of eyewitnesses on a motorway from an accident during Thursday's first hours in which the woman deliberately opted out of more than one car.
The accident occurred on an A1 motorway in a Vaud canton near Morges shortly after midnight.
A woman committed her desperate act by lying on a dark road, hit by more than one car, according to Vaud police in a press release.
The victim was a 49-year-old Swiss woman, and her quest was determined to commit suicide.
A woman died at the scene.
Police say many motorists involved in the accident have already been interviewed, but some have not yet to be identified. Police are appealing against them or other witnesses to the accident to come forward.
Lawyer Gilles Hofstetter tells Le Tribune de Geneve that a forensic investigation has been launched, a logical step in such a situation.
It remains to be seen whether there will be charges being filed against motorists involved in the accident.
“Would a collision be unavoidable?
'It's left for prosecutors to find out," says Hofstetter.
Over the past five years, there have been two similar incidents suggesting that motorists are not held accountable.
According to a newspaper in both incidents, a person was walking on a dark motorway and the court ruled that motorists can't wait to collide with a pedestrian on a freeway in the middle of the night.
Last year, a police official acquitted of manslaughter after colliding with a man lying on an A1 motorway near Paynerne one night in August of 2014.
The tribunal heard the 22-year-old victim had consumed plenty of alcohol and passed out on the road.
The tribunal ruled that the driving style of the police, who was responding to a distress call, was justified and the man lying on the road was an "comprehensibly exceptional and unpredictable situation".
Anyone aware of an accident near Morges on Thursday will be asked to call police on 021 644 83 46 or 021 644 44.
NASA Climatic Model: Venus Has Reliable
