NASA's Goddard Institute for Space Studies (GISS) developed a model to study Venus' past with the same tools and algorithms used by climate scientists.
The model was designed to find out whether the conditions in Venus may have sometimes been similar to those on Earth, even though Venus is hot and an anhydrous on the surface and its throttle is almost entirely made up of carbon dioxide.
According to the researchers' previous performances, Venus' slow rotational speed is explained by its thick atmosphere.
However, based on the latest simulation, it can be assumed that Venus has had a thin-faceted atmosphere similar to the Earth, and it has rotated slowly.
The model was also used to figure out early Venus topography.
There were likely to be more dry areas on the planet than on the Earth, but there may have been enough water and geographic variety to sustain life and prevent rapid evaporation.
Anticipating Venus also benefited from the sun, 30 per cent fainter.
"In GISS modelling, Venus rotates slowly and its half-dawn per sun is nearly two months at a time at a length of two months at a time," GISS researcher Anthony Del Genio said in a press release.
This heats the surface and generates rain, which creates a thick layer of cloud. A layer of cloud acts like an umbrella and protects the surface at a large part of the sun's warmth.
As a result, global average climate temperatures are up to a few degrees lower than Earth's current temperatures.
The model was used to create the story of Venus, which corresponds to previous assumptions of planetary scientists for its development.
Even though Venus may have been viable, for several billion years it has been exposed to excessive heat and ultraviolet light.
Its oceans evaporated, and ultraviolet light burned vaporized water.
As hydrogen, only a thick carbon dioxide-containing thrombotic atmosphere and a deadly greenhouse effect remained in space, i.e. Venus as it is today.
Investigators reported the results of a new Venus climatic modelling in the journal Geophysical Research Letters.
Patrick Roy relinquishes his seat as Avalanche coach
Patrick Roy resigned from his duties on Thursday as coach of the Colorado Avalanche team and deputy president of club hockey operations citing a lack of influence in the team's decision-making.
Joe Sakic, vice president/Director-Currepreneur of Colorado, bolstered the news, announcing that the club would immediately start applying for a new coach.
"For the past three years, I've carried out my role as head coach of the Colorado Avalanche squad and deputy president of the club's hockey operations vigorously, passionately and resolutely," the 50-year-old Roy said in a statement.
"During the summer, I've been pondering a long and fertile consideration of how I would be able to improve the squad, give it the necessary depth and raise it to a higher level.
In order for this to be achieved, the vice-principal president of ice hockey at the club needs to be fully aligned with the organization.
He also needs to have a say in decisions that affect the team's performance.
These conditions are currently not met.”
Sakic, who hired Roy on May 23, 2013, thanked Roy for his contribution to the squad.
"Patrick told me about his decision today," Sakic says.
"We're grateful for everything he's done for our organization, and we wish him all the best for the future."
Roy was awarded the Jack Adams Award as the league's best-of-the-scensor coach of the year for his first season as Avalanche head coach from 2013 to 2014.
Under his leadership, the club reached 112 points and won the middle division, but Colorado lost to Minnesota in the play-offs and hasn't made it to an extended season in the past two years.
The Avalanche achieved 39-39-4 power-play points in 2015-2016 and trailed by five spots from the Minnesota Wild Wild in the Western Conference.
Hall of Fame goaltender Roy won four Stanley Cups in his career – two with the Montreal Canadiens and two with Avalanche.
