Kepler measures star rotation velocity in Pleiades star cluster
The latest mission of the Kepler Space Telescope has been to measure star rotational speed, which has helped astronomers assemble the most comprehensive list of rotation rates of the Plejades' star cluster stars to date.
Based on new information, astronomers will be able to find out why the stars in the same cluster rotate at different speeds and how detivities are associated with planetary formation.
"By comparing the results to other clusters, we will learn to learn more about the relationship between star weight and age and even the history of its solar system," said Luisa Rebull, a researcher at the University of Caladena's Center for Infrared Processing and Analysing in Pasadena, in a press release.
The Pleiadic Star Cretace is located 4,445 light-hundred years from the Earth.
A number of star cluster stars were born about 125 million years ago, which means they're just reaching their adult lives.
They're spinning faster now than ever.
With the rotational rate at its peak, large amounts of star wind erupt from growing stars.
These winds will have a braking effect on the stars as they progress through the magnetic fields of stars.
As the star abounds, its rotation velocity begins to decelerate.
Kepler measures the rotational speed of each star by tracking the time between the appearance of recognisable star spots (corresponding to sunglasses).
The latest listed rotational speed data suggests larger stars are spinning more slowly than smaller ones.
Rebull compares the stars to the dancers – bigger spins slower than the smaller ones.
"We'll see from Pleiades "dancing" that those slowly rotate are typically larger, while the fastest rotating are typically very light stars," says Rebull.
Because Plejades' star cluster is so immovable, it acts as an ideal space lab to compare star properties relative to rotation speeds.
"Plejadi star cluster provides a pillar of support for theoretical models about rotation of both younger and older stars," says Rebull.
We would like to find out more about how, when and why the stars are slowing their rotational speed and end their "dances."
Rebull and his colleagues present their latest analysis of Plejadi's rotation rates in three new papers to be published shortly in Astronomical Journal.
